[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.600]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from June 5th, 2013 titled "What Drives
[00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:10.480]   SaaS Company Valuation? Growth." If you've ever wondered what drives the valuation of a SaaS
[00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:13.680]   vendor, then take a look at this chart that a banker showed me the other day.
[00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:18.400]   The answer, pretty clearly, is revenue growth. The correlation is stunning. Taking some points
[00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:23.840]   off the line, 10% growth gets you an on-premises, like valuation of 2 times forward revenues.
[00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:29.440]   20% growth gets you 3 times 30% growth, gets you 4 times 50% growth, gets you nearly 6 times.
[00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:34.560]   Basically, a growth rate percent slash 10 slash 10. You might think that profitability
[00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:39.200]   played some role in the valuation equation, but if you did, you're wrong. Let's demonstrate
[00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:44.240]   this by looking at CY13 EBITDA margins as reported by the same banker.
[00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:50.880]   Marketo MKTO 44% with the E4 times revenue. Multiple Marin Software, MRI in 40% with the
[00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:57.040]   E4 times revenue. Multiple Workday W-Day, 22% with the E4 times revenue. Multiple Bizarvoi,
[00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:03.280]   SPE, 6% with the E5 times revenue. Multiple Cornerstone on demand, CISOD, 0% with the
[00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:09.040]   E8 times revenue. Multiple Quite Technologies, QLIKE, 13% with the E8 E20 times revenue.
[00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:13.520]   Multiple Tango, TNGO, 17% with the E20 times revenue multiple.
[00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:16.960]   As you can see, there's basically no reward for profitability. In real estate,
[00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:22.160]   what matters is location, location-location, location-location. In SaaS, it's growth,
[00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:28.240]   growth, and growth, and growth. This audio version was created by Grant Duncan and AI
[00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:29.600]   with permission from Dave.
