[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.560]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from June 12th, 2015 titled "The Second
[00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:08.480]   Agenda - Why No Executive Should Ever Have One."
[00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:11.360]   Sometimes leaders have second agendas, CEO.
[00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:14.960]   I want to win for my shareholders and prove I can take a company public.
[00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:19.280]   Founder, I want to win for my shareholders and destroy the great evil at Microsoft.
[00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:22.600]   Volleyball coach, I want to win the league and prove to the world that I can convert
[00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:25.200]   an average outside hitter into a great libro.
[00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.760]   CMO, I want to beat the planned targets and develop my protege.
[00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:32.040]   I'm going to argue that in basically all cases, these are bad.
[00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:33.040]   Why?
[00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:36.420]   Because when a leader has two primary agendas, they can come into conflict.
[00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:41.520]   The CEO will turn down a potentially great buyout offer because he/she personally wants
[00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.800]   to ring the bell at the NYSE.
[00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:47.240]   The founder will turn down a fantastic deal from Microsoft because he does not want to
[00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:49.720]   do business with the great perceived evil.
[00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:53.440]   The volleyball coach might lose the game by not playing the best players to win it.
[00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:57.880]   The CMO might miss planned targets by focusing more on his or her protege than on delivering
[00:00:57.880 --> 00:00:59.700]   MQLs to sales.
[00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:03.680]   This is, of course, not to argue that leaders can only focus on one goal.
[00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:07.360]   Running a company requires a whole set of goals that map across the organ.
[00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:08.360]   Isation.
[00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:12.960]   But leaders should have one mission, one cause, one agenda to win.
[00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:17.160]   Any other agenda, no matter how well-intentioned, will eventually come into conflict with winning
[00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:19.600]   and start to tear the team apart.
[00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:23.020]   Investors find out a prospective buyer was snuffed without due consideration and lose
[00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:27.500]   trust in the CEO and potentially either fire or sue him/her.
[00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:31.520]   The founder ends up distanced by his organization, which now sees him as fighting religious wars
[00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:33.620]   instead of running a business.
[00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:37.120]   Employees leave because they wonder what other great deals won't be pursued for non-business
[00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:38.120]   reasons.
[00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:39.120]   Think.
[00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:40.120]   Yahoo.
[00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:43.660]   The volleyball coach is seen as subjective and someone who plays favorites and thus fails
[00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:47.140]   to recruit top players to his or her team in following years.
[00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:51.840]   The CMO is seen as political and his team, starts to distrust his motivations for assigning
[00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:56.840]   projects, leading to general distrust on the team, and the loss of several key players.
[00:01:56.840 --> 00:02:00.780]   I remember one day, years ago, when I felt that our CEO had not been loyal enough to
[00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:01.780]   a teammate.
[00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:06.240]   I thought, "That's shitty, he prioritized winning over loyalty to a long-term colleague,"
[00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:10.120]   and then I thought some more, and then I realized, "That's exactly the kind of CEO you want
[00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:11.120]   to work for."
[00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:15.400]   I'm not saying we should treat people as disposable and that loyalty shouldn't exist.
[00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:20.400]   Managers should be creative and try to find win/win solutions to issues with employees.
[00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:25.440]   But when you can't, when there appears to be no win/win to be had, then no secondary
[00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:29.520]   agendas, even loyalty, should trump the leader's primary objective.
[00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:34.120]   Even loyalty, I view things like eethics and the WAH, not as secondary agendas but as constraints
[00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:35.440]   on the solution.
[00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:39.880]   This audio version was created by Grant Duncan and AI with permission from Dave.
