[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.960]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from September 1, 2015 titled "I Don't
[00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:06.560]   Want to Talk to You Anymore."
[00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:10.440]   One time, back in the day at Business Objects, we were all flying back from Paris to San
[00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:14.280]   Francisco when the plane pulled ten feet back from the gate and then stopped.
[00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:17.200]   The pilot announced that we were taking a delay of several hours.
[00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:21.880]   Frustrated, one of our board members, a very polished, powerful, statuesque man immediately
[00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:24.140]   asked the flight attendant if he could get off the plane.
[00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:26.900]   He wanted to take another flight and felt unfairly trapped.
[00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:27.900]   She said no.
[00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:32.440]   The light dispute ensued, as we, the management team, watched in awe of his calm yet firm
[00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:33.800]   argumentative style.
[00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:35.000]   A strange thing happened.
[00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.000]   "I don't want to talk to you anymore," he said.
[00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:38.160]   "I want to speak to the pilot."
[00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:40.500]   "But, but, but what do you mean?
[00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:42.920]   You need to talk to me, because uh, uh, uh.
[00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:45.320]   I don't want to talk to you anymore," he repeated.
[00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:49.000]   For years, many of us on the executive team would joke about how one day our terminations
[00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:50.120]   might go down.
[00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:51.200]   But you don't understand.
[00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:54.400]   The seminar attendance was low because there was a blizzard that closed the roads and shut
[00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.320]   down public transportation.
[00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:59.600]   All I know is we failed to achieve our lead generation goal.
[00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:01.080]   But it was a freak April snowstorm.
[00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:03.000]   "I don't want to talk to you anymore."
[00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:07.360]   "But, but, but, uh, uh, I don't want to talk to you anymore."
[00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:09.720]   The phrase developed a certain legend status to it.
[00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:11.600]   I'd forgotten about it for years.
[00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:15.560]   Until one day, at Mark Logic, I was supposed to meet with one of my direct reports and I
[00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:16.560]   didn't want to.
[00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:17.960]   I wasn't looking forward to it.
[00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:20.280]   I didn't want to talk to the person anymore.
[00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:21.880]   And then, a huge gut check went off.
[00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:22.880]   Wait a minute.
[00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:25.920]   What does it mean when I don't want to talk to the person who runs at my company?
